{
  "id": "COMPOUND:4343 - PROTEIN:5845",
  "verbalization": "Cycloleucine is implicated in the relationship with SNU1 through a series of molecular interactions involving sialyltransferases and their targets. The evidence suggests that Cycloleucine acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide, Hydroxy-Alpha-Sanshool, and Khrinone C, all of which are known to interact with the CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase. This enzyme is a key player in the synthesis of sialic acid, a critical component of glycoproteins and glycolipids. The fact that multiple compounds target this enzyme and ultimately lead to SNU1 suggests a potential pathway where Cycloleucine may influence SNU1 through the modulation of sialyltransferase activity. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of sialyltransferases in mediating the effects of Cycloleucine on SNU1, emphasizing the importance of this enzyme in the biological context.",
